## Where each one stands

The desk closed #4471 on 5 October with an engineering update:

- **Parser** ([ticket](https://github.com/JakSko/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1), closed):
  fixed, "fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales".
- **Form hint** ([ticket](https://github.com/JakSko/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2), closed):
  won't fix, "booking-v3 form retired".
- **One day early** ([ticket](https://github.com/JakSko/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3), open):
  not in the update. Reading dates day first does not change a date that moves by one day, so these
  reports stay open.

## How to tell them apart

- **Parser**: a day-first tenant's date is read month first, so day and month trade places. Any
  channel: app, email, and the form on tenants that do not run booking-v3.
- **Form hint**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, and people type what it says.
  Only through the form, only on booking-v3 tenants. The bodies read like parser reports; the channel
  and the form tell them apart.
- **One day early**: the weekday is off by one, or two time zones see two days. No day and month
  swap. The desk already had these as one-day moves in `desk-notes.json`.

## How this was counted

Every report in `reports-4471.json`, once per id (the export pages overlap), counted the desk's way:
each report under the cause it is filed by (`causes.ts`: tenant locale, booking form and channel, not
the subject line or the intake tag), and the desk's one-day moves also as their own problem,
footnoted, as the desk does on the incident page. Then the bodies were read: each names a booked date
and a shown date that fit its problem. One report on the one-day list has no dates; it stays there on
the desk's reading.

## Data

Built from the export of 2026-10-04 (`src/lib/data/reports-4471.json`) at render time; the status
column is the desk's engineering update of 5 October.

